s than three square feet of floor  space per prisoner things are dirty  and the food was pretty bad and  you know protein came from the insects  you were  largely thought of as dangerous a  traitor a race traitor i was just doing  what the spirit said do and the movement  became like a family we always had each  other's back this iconic photo snapped  on may 28 1963 shows the violence that  family endured while trying to integrate  a woolworth's lunch counter in jackson  mississippi memphis norman was being  pulled off his stool and kicked severely  and  the girls were pulled down it quickly  became obvious that i would be safer  sitting  on the empty stool where memphis had  been than i would in the crowd her faith  blocked fear and constant reminders of  the dangers the protester and freedom  rider faced surround the now  grandmother's home i had  a copy of my birth record  which was good for if you have to  identify  a body if someone gets killed it's a  form of identity even in all that  you weren't afraid  no  i figured the lord will see you through  joan's son loki is the founder and  executive director of the joan trump  hour mulholland foundation which hopes  to wipe out racism through education do  you feel that your foundation and  organizations that exist like it do you  think that they're needed even more now  you know the shooting in buffalo proves  it we spoke with the family just days  after the racially motivated attack in  buffalo as it was happening my mother  and the other freedom riders were on  stage in anniston alabama  talking about  the freedom rides and talking about the  bus that was bombed i got back to the  hotel my mind was just  was this on this going  you know  what more should i have done  what more do i need to do  for mulholland that work should be done  by everyone those wise with gray hair  and the young and passionate with bright  eyes i'm happy i did it i had the  opportunity and friendships we made back  then are good to the end but  more and more ends are coming  now joan trump hour mulholland's life is  expected to be the center of a feature  film produced by award-winning actor  anthony mackie the family says the film  is in development right now but hopes it  will be done at some point next year  which is the 60th anniversary of many  civil rights movement event and what a  life what a story to tell and that her  mother what she learned we talk about  learning hate but what her mother taught  she didn't take and she still found this  path she didn't take and she started out  as a freshman at duke and said that when  she first started participating in these  sit-ins college administrators wanted  her to undergo a psychiatric evaluation  that's just how  much the the community did not believe  in what she was doing and she's oh my  gosh she's a sorority member delta sigma  theta her son is an omega sci-fi  fraternity member yes they're amazing  they surround themselves with people who  everyone i when we were leaving i asked  i looked at her and i said thank you for  everything that you did and she replied  i did it for all of us which definitely  shows that you know when people are not  equal everyone is harmed amazing amazing  job what a wonderful story thank you
 

